<h2 id="chap6sec1" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="9B4082">6.1</span> $\infty $-Topoi: Definitions and Characterizations</h2>


<p>
Before we study the $\infty $-categorical version of topos theory, it seems appropriate to briefly review the classical theory. Recall that a <em>topos</em> is a category $\calC $ which behaves like the category of sets, or (more generally) the category of sheaves of sets on a topological space. There are several (equivalent) ways of making this idea precise. The following result is proved (in a slightly different form) in <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/SGA">SGA</a>]</span>: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="EBB0AE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/EBB0AE">Proposition <span data-tag="EBB0AE">6.1.0.1</span>.</a>topos Let $\calC $ be a category. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(A)$"><p>
The category $\calC $ is $($equivalent to$)$ the category of sheaves of sets on some Grothendieck site. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(B)$"><p>
The category $\calC $ is $($equivalent to$)$ a left-exact localization of the category of presheaves of sets on some small category $\calC _0$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(C)$"><p>
Giraud's axioms!for ordinary topoi Giraud's axioms are satisfied: 
</p>
<ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
The category $\calC $ is presentable $($that is, $\calC $ has small colimits and a set of small generators$)$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(ii)$"><p>
Colimits in $\calC $ are universal. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iii)$"><p>
Coproducts in $\calC $ are disjoint. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iv)$"><p>
Equivalence relations in $\calC $ are effective. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="8F7A99">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/8F7A99">Definition <span data-tag="8F7A99">6.1.0.2</span>.</a> A category $\calC $ is called a <em>topos</em> if it satisfies the equivalent conditions of Proposition <a href="/tag/EBB0AE" data-tag="EBB0AE">6.1.0.1</a>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">6.1.0.3</span>.</a>A reader who is unfamiliar with some of the terminology used in the statement of Proposition <a href="/tag/EBB0AE" data-tag="EBB0AE">6.1.0.1</a> should not worry: we will review the meaning of each condition in §<a href="/tag/D30B10" data-tag="D30B10">6.1.1</a> as we search for $\infty $-categorical generalizations of axioms $(i)$ through $(iv)$. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
Our goal in this section is to introduce the $\infty $-categorical analogue of Definition <a href="/tag/8F7A99" data-tag="8F7A99">6.1.0.2</a>. Proposition <a href="/tag/EBB0AE" data-tag="EBB0AE">6.1.0.1</a> suggests several possible approaches. We begin with the simplest of these: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="5B5BBE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/5B5BBE">Definition <span data-tag="5B5BBE">6.1.0.4</span>.</a>$\infty $-topos Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-category. We will say that $\calX $ is an <em>$\infty $-topos</em> if there exists a small $\infty $-category $\calC $ and an accessible left exact localization functor $\calP (\calC ) \rightarrow \calX $. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">6.1.0.5</span>.</a>Definition <a href="/tag/5B5BBE" data-tag="5B5BBE">6.1.0.4</a> involves an accessibility condition which was not mentioned in Proposition <a href="/tag/EBB0AE" data-tag="EBB0AE">6.1.0.1</a>. This is because every left exact localization of a category of <em>set</em>-valued presheaves is automatically accessible (see Proposition <a href="/tag/7E6C7C" data-tag="7E6C7C">6.4.3.9</a>). We do not know if the corresponding result holds for $\SSet $-valued presheaves. However, it is true under a suitable hypercompleteness assumption: see <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/toenvezz">toenvezz</a>]</span>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
Adopting Definition <a href="/tag/5B5BBE" data-tag="5B5BBE">6.1.0.4</a> amounts to selecting an <em>extrinsic</em> approach to higher topos theory: the class of $\infty $-topoi is defined to be the smallest collection of $\infty $-categories which contains $\SSet $ and is stable under certain constructions (left exact localizations and the formation of functor categories). The main objective of this section is to give several reformulations of Definition <a href="/tag/8F7A99" data-tag="8F7A99">6.1.0.2</a> which have a more intrinsic flavor. Our results may be summarized in the following statement (all our our terminology will be explained later in this section): 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-theorem" id="5A32AA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/5A32AA">Theorem <span data-tag="5A32AA">6.1.0.6</span>.</a>Giraud's theorem!for $\infty $-topoi Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-category. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The $\infty $-category $\calX $ is an $\infty $-topos. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The $\infty $-category $\calX $ is presentable, and for every small simplicial set $K$ and every natural transformation $\overline{\alpha }: \overline{p} \rightarrow \overline{q}$ of diagrams $\overline{p}, \overline{q}: K^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \calX $, the following condition is satisfied: 
</p>
<ul>
  <li><p>
If $\overline{q}$ is a colimit diagram and $\alpha = \overline{\alpha } | K$ is a Cartesian transformation, then $\overline{p}$ is a colimit diagram if and only if $\overline{\alpha }$ is a Cartesian transformation. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
The $\infty $-category $\calX $ satisfies the following $\infty $-categorical analogues of Giraud's axioms: 
</p>
<ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
The $\infty $-category $\calX $ is presentable. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(ii)$"><p>
Colimits in $\calX $ are universal. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iii)$"><p>
Coproducts in $\calX $ are disjoint. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iv)$"><p>
Every groupoid object of $\calX $ is effective. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
We will review the meanings of conditions $(i)$ through $(iv)$ in §<a href="/tag/D30B10" data-tag="D30B10">6.1.1</a> and §<a href="/tag/EDAD73" data-tag="EDAD73">6.1.2</a>. In §<a href="/tag/F30EB9" data-tag="F30EB9">6.1.3</a> we will give several equivalent formulations of $(2)$, and prove the implications $(1) \Rightarrow (2) \Rightarrow (3)$. The implication $(3) \Rightarrow (1)$ is the most difficult; we will give the proof in §<a href="/tag/EDD072" data-tag="EDD072">6.1.5</a> after establishing a crucial technical lemma in §<a href="/tag/C9F9BC" data-tag="C9F9BC">6.1.4</a>. Finally, in §<a href="/tag/147708" data-tag="147708">6.1.6</a> we will establish yet another characterization of $\infty $-topoi, based on the theory of classifying objects. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/">Remark <span data-tag="">6.1.0.7</span>.</a>The characterization of the class of $\infty $-topoi given by part $(2)$ of Theorem <a href="/tag/5A32AA" data-tag="5A32AA">6.1.0.6</a> is due to Rezk, as are many of the ideas presented in §<a href="/tag/F30EB9" data-tag="F30EB9">6.1.3</a>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ of Theorem <a href="/tag/5A32AA" data-tag="5A32AA">6.1.0.6</a> can be viewed as an $\infty $-categorical analogue of the equivalence $(B) \Leftrightarrow (C)$ in Proposition <a href="/tag/EBB0AE" data-tag="EBB0AE">6.1.0.1</a>. It is natural to ask if there is also some equivalent of the characterization $(A)$. To put the question another way: given a small $\infty $-category $\calC $, does there exist some natural description of the class of all left-exact localizations of $\calC $? Experience with classical topos theory suggests that we might try to characterize such localizations in terms of <em>Grothendieck topologies</em> on $\calC $. We will introduce a theory of Grothendieck topologies on $\infty $-categories in §<a href="/tag/DD783A" data-tag="DD783A">6.2.2</a>, and show that every Grothendieck topology on $\calC $ determines a left-exact localization of $\calP (\calC )$. However, it turns out that not every $\infty $-topos arises via this construction. This raises a natural question: is it possible to give an explicit description of <em>all</em> left-exact localizations of $\calP (\calC )$, perhaps in terms of some more refined theory of Grothendieck topologies? We will give a partial answer to this question in §<a href="/tag/59F259" data-tag="59F259">6.5</a>. 
</p>
